<?php
declare(strict_types=1);

namespace Infrastructure\Legacy;

/**
 * A miniature Active Record base class.
 *
 * SUPPLIED BY THE COURSE. You do not edit this file.
 *
 * WHY THE COURSE SHIPS ITS OWN INSTEAD OF USING ELOQUENT OR DOCTRINE
 * -------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Two reasons, and neither is that a real ORM would be too complicated.
 *
 * FIRST, the lesson is framework-neutral on purpose. A challenge written
 * against Eloquent teaches Eloquent's API, and half the people taking this
 * course are on Symfony. What actually transfers is a decision about where
 * rules live, and that decision is identical in both.
 *
 * SECOND, this file is sixty lines and you can read all of it. Eloquent's
 * Model is several thousand across a dozen traits, and the point being made
 * here would be somewhere inside it.
 *
 * WHAT IT REPRODUCES, FAITHFULLY
 * -------------------------------
 * The three things that make an Active Record model hard to put rules on, and
 * every one of them is present in the real thing:
 *
 *   1. MAGIC ATTRIBUTE ACCESS. `$record->status` reads a column. There is no
 *      property to make private, no method to override, and no compiler
 *      involvement of any kind.
 *
 *   2. MAGIC ASSIGNMENT. `$record->status = 'invoiced'` writes a column, with
 *      no validation and no way for the class to object — unless it takes over
 *      __set, which is what the challenge is about.
 *
 *   3. THE ROW IS THE OBJECT. It has an identity because it has a primary key,
 *      it knows how to save itself, and it can be constructed in any state at
 *      all, including states the business forbids.
 *
 * WHAT IT LEAVES OUT
 * -------------------
 * Relations, eager loading, scopes, events, soft deletes, mass assignment
 * guards, and a query builder. All of those matter in practice and none of
 * them changes the argument.
 *
 * The one omission worth naming: a real ORM can HYDRATE an object without
 * calling your constructor, which is why reconstitution (Lesson 3.2) is a
 * problem you inherit rather than choose. `hydrate()` below does the same
 * thing, so the challenge meets it honestly.
 */
abstract class ActiveRecord
{
    /**
     * The row, as columns. Protected rather than public, which is already one
     * step further than several real ORMs go.
     *
     * @var array<string, scalar|null>
     */
    protected array $attributes = [];

    /** The table, keyed by primary key. A database, for the purposes of this lesson. */
    protected static array $storage = [];

    /**
     * @param array<string, scalar|null> $attributes
     */
    public function __construct(array $attributes = [])
    {
        $this->attributes = $attributes;
    }

    /**
     * Straight out of the ORM playbook: build the object WITHOUT running the
     * constructor's logic, because the row is already valid by definition.
     *
     * This is the reconstitution problem from Lesson 3.2, arriving as a
     * property of the framework rather than as a design decision. You do not
     * get to opt out of it.
     *
     * @param array<string, scalar|null> $row
     */
    public static function hydrate(array $row): static
    {
        $record = new static();
        $record->attributes = $row;

        return $record;
    }

    public function __get(string $name): mixed
    {
        return $this->attributes[$name] ?? null;
    }

    public function __set(string $name, mixed $value): void
    {
        $this->attributes[$name] = $value;
    }

    public function __isset(string $name): bool
    {
        return isset($this->attributes[$name]);
    }

    /** @return array<string, scalar|null> */
    public function toRow(): array
    {
        return $this->attributes;
    }

    public function save(): void
    {
        $key = (string) ($this->attributes['id'] ?? '');

        if ($key === '') {
            throw new \RuntimeException('Cannot save a record with no id.');
        }

        static::$storage[static::class][$key] = $this->attributes;
    }

    public static function find(string $id): ?static
    {
        $row = static::$storage[static::class][$id] ?? null;

        return $row === null ? null : static::hydrate($row);
    }

    public static function truncate(): void
    {
        static::$storage[static::class] = [];
    }
}
